blind_proposals.content:
Everyone is reading the Oleshky ceasefire as a peace signal. I see it as a proof of concept for a local humanitarian pause, not a runway to a nationwide one. From here I get four branches. The biggest is “truce creep”: local deals and sectoral arrangements continue, but the mid-November trilateral talks produce working groups and limited understandings, not a front-wide stop. I put that at 55%. The second is “no deal, continued war”: Russia keeps pressing in Donetsk and Zaporizhzhia and rejects a full ceasefire as it has been signalling, while talks resume and collapse. That is 30%. The third is the only YES branch: a post-midterm US push forces a joint Russian-Ukrainian statement of a nationwide ceasefire with a start date, likely tied to a prisoner exchange and energy infrastructure. That is 12%. The fourth is a crisis or military shock that either forces a broader truce or scuttles talks; 3%. The probability of YES is clustered in those last two branches, roughly 10 to 15%.

The key uncertainty is Moscow’s incentive. Russia is still advancing in some sectors. A nationwide ceasefire would freeze the front before territorial terms are settled, which is exactly the outcome Ukraine wants. Russia may prefer limited deals such as the Black Sea or energy truces that relieve pressure without giving up offensive initiative. The Oleshky mission is working, but scaling from one city to the whole 1,200-kilometer front requires monitoring, violation consequences, and a political trade that is not on the table. I would raise my estimate if the mid-November talks become a leaders’ summit and Russia stops rejecting the full ceasefire concept. I would lower it if the next phase is only a Black Sea or energy truce. My number is 8-18.

